### How do you define a Supplier?

A Supplier has no arguments and returns a value each time get() is called.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.function.Supplier;
import java.util.Optional;

public class SupplierDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Supplier<Employee> defaultEmployee =
            () -> new Employee("UNKNOWN", "Unassigned", 0.0);

        // Called only when needed
        Employee e = defaultEmployee.get();
        System.out.println(e.getName()); // UNKNOWN

        // Used with Optional.orElseGet
        Optional<Employee> empty = Optional.empty();
        Employee fallback = empty.orElseGet(defaultEmployee);
        System.out.println(fallback.getName()); // UNKNOWN
    }
}
```

```text
UNKNOWN
UNKNOWN
```

### What is the difference between orElse and orElseGet?

| Method | When default is evaluated | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| orElse(value) | Always, even when Optional has a value | Default is cheap and pre-computed |
| orElseGet(supplier) | Only when Optional is empty | Default is expensive or has side-effects |

```java
// WRONG: expensive default created even when not needed
Optional<Employee> present = Optional.of(existingEmployee);
Employee e1 = present.orElse(expensiveDbLookup());
// expensiveDbLookup() was called for nothing!

// CORRECT: lazy evaluation
Employee e2 = present.orElseGet(() -> expensiveDbLookup());
// expensiveDbLookup() is NOT called because present has a value
```

### How do you use Supplier with Stream.generate?

```java
import java.util.stream.Stream;

// Generate 5 default employees
Stream.generate(defaultEmployee)
    .limit(5)
    .forEach(e -> System.out.println(e.getName()));
```

```text
UNKNOWN
UNKNOWN
UNKNOWN
UNKNOWN
UNKNOWN
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: confusing orElse with orElseGet.

```java
// Both return the same result, but orElse always evaluates the argument:
Optional<Employee> opt = Optional.of(employee);
opt.orElse(new Employee("Default"));       // Employee created, then discarded
opt.orElseGet(() -> new Employee("Default")); // Lambda never called
```

### What about BooleanSupplier and other specialised suppliers?

Java provides specialised suppliers for primitives to avoid boxing.

```java
import java.util.function.IntSupplier;

IntSupplier accountCounter = () -> 1001; // no boxing
int id = accountCounter.getAsInt();
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Supplier defers construction: you pay the cost only when the value is consumed.
- Use supplier-form logging (log.info(() -> msg)) so the message string is assembled only when the level is enabled.
- Supplier is commonly used in collector factories, map computation (computeIfAbsent), and lazy initialization.
- A Supplier can return a different value on each call (new object) or always the same (cached value).

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the difference between Supplier and Callable?" Callable can throw checked exceptions; Supplier cannot.
- "Can a Supplier take arguments?" No; use Function for one argument or BiFunction for two.
- "How do you create a Supplier from a constructor?" Employee::new serves as Supplier<Employee> when matching the no-arg constructor.
